# The Azure RBAC Formula

You have now created two Azure role assignments.

The first is:

```text
PRINCIPAL
SEIR_Developers

        +

ROLE
Contributor

        +

SCOPE
SEIR-DEV

        =

ACCESS
Manage resources inside SEIR-DEV
```

The second is:

```text
PRINCIPAL
SEIR_Prod_Readers

        +

ROLE
Reader

        +

SCOPE
SEIR-PROD

        =

ACCESS
View resources inside SEIR-PROD
```

That is the fundamental Azure RBAC model.

---

# Understanding Scope

Azure RBAC permissions can be assigned at different levels.

Conceptually:

```text
Management Group
       |
       v
Subscription
       |
       v
Resource Group
       |
       v
Individual Resource
```

Permissions assigned higher in the hierarchy can apply to resources beneath that scope.

For example:

```text
Contributor
     @
Subscription
```

can affect:

```text
Subscription
    |
    +---- SEIR-DEV
    |
    +---- SEIR-PROD
    |
    +---- Other Resource Groups
```

But:

```text
Contributor
     @
SEIR-DEV
```

is limited to:

```text
SEIR-DEV
```

This is why scope matters.

---

# Least Privilege

A basic IAM principle is:

> Give an identity only the permissions required to perform its job, and only at the scope where those permissions are required.

Instead of:

```text
Chewbacca
    |
    v
Contributor @ Entire Subscription
```

we used:

```text
Chewbacca
    |
    v
SEIR_Developers
    |
    v
Contributor @ SEIR-DEV
```

That limits the effective scope.

---

# Why Use Groups?

You could assign the role directly to Chewbacca.

Example:

```text
Chewbacca
     |
     v
Contributor
     |
     v
SEIR-DEV
```

But this becomes difficult to manage when you have many users.

Instead:

```text
Chewbacca ----+
              |
Han Solo -----+----> SEIR_Developers
              |           |
Leia ---------+           |
                          v
                     Contributor
                          |
                          v
                       SEIR-DEV
```

Now access is managed through group membership.

If Han Solo joins the development team:

```text
Add Han Solo
      |
      v
SEIR_Developers
```

He inherits the access associated with the group.

If Chewbacca leaves the team:

```text
Remove Chewbacca
       |
       v
SEIR_Developers
```

His group-based access is removed.

